<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boudica</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 60 or 61</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Boadicea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boudica was queen of the Iceni of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> who led a great revolt against Roman rule in 60 or 61 CE after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> seized her late husband's kingdom and mistreated her family. Her forces destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Colchester</a>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-albans">St Albans</a>), killing many thousands, before the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Gaius Suetonius Paulinus</a> defeated them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>. She died soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boudica.jpg" alt="Boudica" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Opie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Boudica_by_John_Opie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Boudica was the consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasutagus">Prasutagus</a>, king of the Iceni, a tribe living in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> and parts of neighbouring counties. The Iceni had revolted against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 47 and afterwards kept their independence. Prasutagus ruled as a nominally independent ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and had two daughters with Boudica. In his will he made both the daughters and the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> his heirs. The name may be an honorific title, and the name she bore for most of her life is unknown.</p><p>Accounts of the revolt come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>. Dio wrote the longest, about 140 years after her death, and his text survives only in an epitome by the 11th-century monk John Xiphilinus. His details are often fictitious, and the speeches both Tacitus and Dio give her were never recorded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasutagus">Prasutagus</a> died, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> ignored his will and absorbed the kingdom into the province of Britannia. Tacitus tells of pillaging and of the flogging of Boudica and rape of her daughters. Dio mentions none of this and gives other causes, among them the recall of loans by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>. The Iceni and the Trinovantes raised an army reported at 120,000 men and attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a>, a colony for retired soldiers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-ix-hispana">Legio IX Hispana</a> tried to relieve the town and was overwhelmed.</p><p>The governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Gaius Suetonius Paulinus</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> before the rebels but, outnumbered, abandoned it, and they burned it. They also sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a>. Modern estimates put the dead at 70,000 to 80,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and Britons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> gathered nearly 10,000 men and took a stand in a narrow pass with a wood behind. His javelins, wedge formation and cavalry crushed the larger British army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> says that Boudica poisoned herself, while Dio says she fell ill and died and was given a lavish burial. The crisis made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> consider withdrawing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, but the victory confirmed Roman control of the province. Interest in Boudica revived in the English Renaissance and brought her fame in the Victorian era.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boudica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 60 or 61, while the governor Gaius Suetonius Paulinus was attacking Anglesey, <b>Boudica</b> of the Iceni rose in revolt and destroyed Camulodunum, Londinium and Verulamium before she was defeated somewhere in the Midlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Women of high rank could hold power: Cartimandua ruled the Brigantes of northern Britain, and <b>Boudica</b> led the Iceni in revolt.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>When Prasutagus died around 60, leaving half his realm to Nero, Roman officials seized it, flogged his widow <b>Boudica</b> and raped her daughters.</blockquote><a href="/state/iceni">Iceni</a></li><li><blockquote>Anglesey, the island of Mona, lay on their borders and was a centre of the druids, whose sanctuary Suetonius Paulinus attacked in 60 before <b>Boudica's</b> revolt recalled him.</blockquote><a href="/state/ordovices">Ordovices</a></li><li><blockquote>Resentment led the Trinovantes to join <b>Boudica</b> of the Iceni in 60 or 61, when Camulodunum was burned.</blockquote><a href="/state/trinovantes">Trinovantes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Roman Britain. Died in Roman Britain. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/prasutagus"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Prasutagus</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; d. c. 60</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Roman lust has gone so far that not our very persons, nor even age or virginity, are left unpolluted. But heaven is on the side of a righteous vengeance; a legion which dared to fight has perished…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to the Britons before battle (Tacitus, Annals, book 14) &#183; tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb, 1876 &#183; Tacitus's version of her words from her chariot, 60 or 61 CE &#183; <a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Tacitus/annals.mb.txt" rel="noopener">The Internet Classics Archive</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If you weigh well the strength of the armies, and the causes of the war, you will see that in this battle you must conquer or die. This is a woman's resolve; as for men, they may live and be slaves.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to the Britons before battle (Tacitus, Annals, book 14) &#183; tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb, 1876 &#183; Tacitus's version of her words from her chariot, 60 or 61 CE &#183; <a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Tacitus/annals.mb.txt" rel="noopener">The Internet Classics Archive</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
